   ```json
   {
     "docker": {
       "image": "node:14.17.0",
       "network": "host",
       "volumes": [
         "cypress:/cypress"
       ],
       "binds": {
         "/path/to/your/project:/app"
       }
     },
     "testFiles": "**/*.spec.js",
     "experimentalInteractiveRun": true,
     "experimentalViewportSnapshots": true,
     "reporter": "mocha-junit-reporter",
     "reporterOptions": {
       "mochaFile": "test-results.xml",
       "pageTitle": "Cypress Test Results",
       "appendable": true,
       "failureSeverity": "error",
       "failureIcon": "x",
       "failureMessage": true,
       "failureTrace": true
     },
     "plugins": [
       "cypress-docker-plugin"
     ]
   }
   ```

   - `docker.image`: Specifies the Docker image to use for testing. In this example, we are using the official Node.js 14.17.0 image.
   - `docker.network`: Sets the Docker network mode to `host` to allow Cypress to interact with the host system.
   - `docker.volumes`: Mounts the Cypress test artifacts directory (`/cypress`) inside the Docker container.
   - `docker.binds`: Maps the project directory to `/app` inside the Docker container.
   - `testFiles`: Specifies the pattern to match for test files. In this example, we are looking for `.spec.js` files.
   - `experimentalInteractiveRun`: Enables interactive mode, allowing you to manually trigger screenshots and videos.
   - `experimentalViewportSnapshots`: Enables capturing screenshots at different viewport sizes.
   - `reporter`: Specifies the reporter to use for test results. In this example, we are using the `mocha-junit-reporter`.
   - `reporterOptions`: Configures the `mocha-junit-reporter`. In this example, we are specifying the file name for the test results XML, the title for the report, and enabling appending to the file.
   - `plugins`: Specifies the Cypress Docker plugin.

5. Create a new Cypress test file named `example.spec.js` and add the following code:

   ```js
   import { defineConfig } from "cypress";

   const captureScreenshot = (name, options) => {
     Cypress.Snapshot.saveSnapshot(
       {
         ...options,
         screenshotName: `${name}.png`,
         capture: "fullPage"
       },
       () => {
         Cypress.log({
           name: "screenshot",
           message: `Saved "${name}.png"`
         });
       }
     );
   };

   export default defineConfig({
     e2e: {
       setupNodeEvents(on, config) {
         // Add Cypress Docker plugin
         require("cypress-docker-plugin")(on, config);

         // Add screenshot capture hook
         on("task", {
           captureScreenshot: (name) => captureScreenshot(name, { fullscreen: true })
         });

         return config;
       },
     },
   });
   ```

   - `captureScreenshot`: A custom task function that captures a full-page screenshot and saves it to the Cypress artifacts directory.
   - `defineConfig`: Configures Cypress for end-to-end testing.
   - `setupNodeEvents`: Sets up Cypress Docker plugin and adds the `captureScreenshot` task.

6. Run Cypress tests by executing the following command:

   ```
   npx cypress run --spec example.spec.js
   ```

   This will start the Docker container, run the Cypress tests, and capture screenshots (if you manually trigger them).

7. To view the captured screenshots, navigate to the Cypress artifacts directory (`/cypress/screenshots`) inside the Docker container:

   ```
   docker exec -it <container_id> ls /cypress/screenshots
   ```

   Replace `<container_id>` with the ID of the Docker container running your Cypress tests.

With these steps, you have created a high-quality testing framework in Cypress that utilizes Docker and captures screenshots. You can customize the configuration further to suit your specific needs.

